  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}\u20613t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t\\,j$ meters. Find the speed of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds.",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{\u2032} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}\u20613t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t\\,j)^{\u2032} \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}\u20613t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, we calculate the magnitude of the velocity vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}\u20613t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}\u20613t+36{cos}^{2}\u20613t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}\u20613t+{cos}^{2}\u20613t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the object is $6\\text{\u00a0m/s}.$\n\nNote that the speed is constant, but the velocity is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\n||v||=R|\\omega |,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the radius of the circle, and $\\omega $ is the angular velocity.\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $R=2\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||v||=2\\cdot |3|=6\\text{\u00a0m/s}.\n$$",
